# Continuum Subtraction is a technique to workaround the
# fact that a narrowband filter not only lets through
# quantum emissions, but also continuous blackbody
# radiation. By using a broadband filter that contains
# the narrowband, we can subtract the background leaving
# an approximation of just the emissions.
#
# The technique seems to originate from a pixinsight
# developer, as documented here:
# https://youtu.be/1Wd7VxpMCH4
#
# To avoid star artifacts, it is best to operate on
# starless images.
#
# This script requires files {rgb,ha,oiii}.fit to exist
# in the working directory, i.e. for use on RGB + Ha/Oiii
# dual narrowband.
#
# Magic numbers are used to define the scaling factors
# between the narrow and broadbands.
#
# The following files are produced: delete what is not
# needed:
#
# - hgb (Ha, G, B)
# - rgo (R, G, Oiii)
# - hgo (Ha, G, Oiii)
# - rgb_with_ha (R + HaCS, G, B + HaCS)
# - rgb_with_oiii (R, G + OiiiCS, B + OiiiCS)
# - rgb_with_ha_and_oiii (R + HaCS, G + OiiiCS, B + HaCS + OiiiCS)
#
# It is common to blend the emission lines into the RGB
# image with a specific colour, e.g. HaCS as purple, OiiiCS
# as cyan.
#
# R: R+Q*(HaCS-med(HaCS))
# B: B+Q*(HaCS-med(HaCS))*.2
#
# B: B + Q*(OIII - med(OIII))
# G: G + Q*(OIII - med(OIII)) * 0.2
#
# Parameter: Q=0.5
#
# See the comments further in the script for how to manually
# intervene with the continuum subtracted image, before it is
# composited back into RGB. Continuum subtraction tends to
# leave a lot of artifacts. In particular, it would be good to be
# able to remove the stars from the subtracted image because the
# pixel math doesn't do a good job of that and the composed final
# image gives the impression of chromatic abberation in the stars.

requires 1.2.0

#setcompress 1 -type=gzip1 0

load rgb
split process/r process/g process/b

load ha
save process/ha

load oiii
save process/oiii

cd process
convert channels
register channels

# siril will use alphabetical ordering, so Ha is
load r_channels_00003
save reg_ha
rgbcomp reg_ha g b -out=../hgb

#stat
#
# and this is Oiii
load r_channels_00004
save reg_oiii
rgbcomp r g reg_oiii -out=../rgo

rgbcomp reg_ha g reg_oiii -out=../hgo

# remove stars before doing continuum subtraction
# (this is the most expensive bit)
load r
starnet -stretch -nostarmask

load reg_ha
starnet -stretch -nostarmask

load b
starnet -stretch -nostarmask

load reg_oiii
starnet -stretch -nostarmask

# at this point, we should probably just use the new python plugin...

# so that we don't have to do it again later. When finding a good
# parameter I tend to prefer to maximise the signal of the nebulous
# regions against the noise of the background, rather than any other
# arbitrary metric. I'm ok if this means pulling in galaxy cores.
pm "$starless_reg_ha$ - ($starless_r$ - med($starless_r$)) * 0.25"
save ha_cs

pm "$starless_reg_oiii$ - ($starless_b$ - med($starless_b$)) * 0.25"
save oiii_cs

# NOTE: you can go back to the _cs files manually and clean them up, e.g.
# with star removal, a manual mask, or noise reduction. If you do that,
# copy the remainder of the script into a scratchpad and run it, to
# recreate the composited images.

# remove the median so we don't have to repeat it out
# every time. Note that I feel we should remove the original
# narrowband median, not the median of the continuum subtracted
# image, but since the difference is negligible, and this is
# more compatible with manually edited _cs files, we use the
# median of the _cs image.
#pm "$ha_cs$ - med($reg_ha$)"
pm "$ha_cs$ - med($ha_cs$)"
save ha_cs_m
#pm "$oiii_cs$ - med($reg_oiii$)"
pm "$oiii_cs$ - med($oiii_cs$)"
save oiii_cs_m

# now for the Ha
pm "$r$ + 0.5 * $ha_cs_m$"
save r_with_ha

pm "$b$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $ha_cs_m$"
save b_with_ha

rgbcomp r_with_ha g b_with_ha -out=../rgb_with_ha

# then the Oiii
pm "$g$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $oiii_cs_m$"
save g_with_oiii

pm "$b$ + 0.5 * $oiii_cs_m$"
save b_with_oiii

rgbcomp r g_with_oiii b_with_oiii -out=../rgb_with_oiii

# all together now
pm "$b$ + 0.5 * $oiii_cs_m$ + 0.5 * 0.2 * $ha_cs_m$"
save b_with_ha_and_oiii

rgbcomp r_with_ha g_with_oiii b_with_ha_and_oiii -out=../rgb_with_ha_and_oiii

cd ..
load rgb_with_ha_and_oiii

#setcompress 0